-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Exploration through SQL
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Let's first explore our TPC-DI dataset through DBSQL, using a series of SQL statements.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### Schemas
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Let's see the schemas (databases) created by the TPC-DI preparation and ingestion process using the `SHOW SCHEMAS` statement. Note that this operates in the currently selected catalog, which in this training environment, is automatically set to your personal catalog.

-- COMMAND ----------

SHOW SCHEMAS

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC The job created two schemas:
-- MAGIC * *tpcdi_dbsql_10*: contains all the TPC-DI data mart tables for downstream consumption
-- MAGIC * *tpcdi_dbsql_10_stage*: contains staging tables (intermediate tables that are used to create the data mart tables)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Note that the *dbsql* component of the names comes from the selection of a SQL warehouse as the compute resource performing the work, and the *10* part is derived from the preselected scale factor.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### Tables
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Let's explore the tables and how some of these are set up. We'll use the *dimcustomer* table as an example, but feel free to explore others as well.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Examining table metadata and information
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The `DESCRIBE` statement can be used to obtain basic information on the table's schema.

-- COMMAND ----------

DESCRIBE tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC As we can see, the table contains a surrogate key *sk_customerid*. A surrogate key is used to uniquely identify a row, but is not naturally occurring within the row data and has to be artificially derived or calculated somehow. It has no meaning from a business standpoint; it's used for database management purposes.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Additional information about how the table is managed can be obtained by adding the `EXTENDED` modifier to the `DESCRIBE` statement.

-- COMMAND ----------

DESCRIBE EXTENDED tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC Additional table information and statistics can be obtained using the `DETAIL` modifier.

-- COMMAND ----------

DESCRIBE DETAIL tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Sampling table data
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC We can take peek at the table data using a simple `SELECT` statement. In this case, we limit the query to 100 rows to keep the query short.

-- COMMAND ----------

SELECT * FROM tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer LIMIT 100

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Table history
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Understanding how a table has changed over time can be an important aspect of managing a data warehouse. Once again, the `DESCRIBE` statement with the `HISTORY` modifier can be used to obtain the table history.

-- COMMAND ----------

DESCRIBE HISTORY tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC Using `VERSION AS OF` as part of your queries allows you to target specific versions from a table's historical record.

-- COMMAND ----------

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer VERSION AS OF 0

-- COMMAND ----------

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer VERSION AS OF 1

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### Data governance
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Let's explore the permissions and how some of these are set up. We'll use the *dimcustomer* table as an example, but feel free to explore others as well.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Exploring permissions
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Let's observe the permissions at various levels of the data hierarchy, beginning with the *dimcustomer* table. For this we use the `SHOW GRANTS` statement.

-- COMMAND ----------

SHOW GRANTS ON tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC Now let's examine the permissions on the containing schema, *tpcdi_dbsql_10*.

-- COMMAND ----------

SHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA tpcdi_dbsql_10

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC The way the learning environment is set up, both responses are identical, since **ALL_PRIVILEGES** is granted at the catalog level, and these permissions are inherited downward; this is an important property of the Unity Catalog security model.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Granting permissions
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Granting permissions allows others to access an object in a specific way, and in SQL is done through the `GRANT` statement.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Note that permissions on an object can only be managed by:
-- MAGIC * A metastore admin
-- MAGIC * The owner of the object
-- MAGIC * Someone who has the `MANAGE` privilege on the object
-- MAGIC * The owner of the catalog or schema containing the object

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ##### Explicit privileges
-- MAGIC Unity Catalog supports two methods for granting privileges: *explicit* and *inherited*.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC With explicit privilege assignment, you assign the privileges directly on the objects to which they apply. Suppose, for example, we wanted to grant `SELECT` on two tables. Following an explicit privilege scheme, we would need to grant the following privileges to the grantee:
-- MAGIC * `SELECT` on the tables
-- MAGIC * `USE SCHEMA` on the containing schema
-- MAGIC * `USE CATALOG` on the containing catalog
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC It can be a lot of work to manage all these permissions, but there's little room for error since we are naming all accessible objects explicitly. In other words, there's very little chance to over-grant permissions when following this approach.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Here's how we would open up the *dimcustomer* table for all to see:

-- COMMAND ----------

GRANT SELECT ON tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer TO `account users`

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC In the Unity Catalog security model though, this isn't sufficient. The grantee needs additional permissions at the schema and catalog levels.

-- COMMAND ----------

GRANT USE_SCHEMA ON SCHEMA tpcdi_dbsql_10 TO `account users`

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC Finally, we'd need a grant similar to the following, however this will not work in the learning environment due to the secure way in which it is set up.
-- MAGIC ```
-- MAGIC GRANT USE_CATALOG ON CATALOG <catalog> TO `account users`
-- MAGIC ```
-- MAGIC Before proceeding, let's recheck the permissions on the `dimcustomer` table:

-- COMMAND ----------

SHOW GRANTS ON tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC As before, you have `ALL_PRIVILEGES` via the containing catalog, but now we also see that all users have `SELECT` on it as well.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ##### Inherited privileges
-- MAGIC As mentioned previously Unity Catalog supports two methods for granting privileges: *explicit* and *inherited*, and we just saw how the explicit model works.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Because it's so explicit, it's a pretty secure way to administer permissions and not very prone to errors or over-granting of permissions. However, it's not very convenient. Suppose we wanted to grant `SELECT` on all the tables.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC This is where inherited privileges can make life easier. Instead of explicitly granting `SELECT` on all the tables, we can grant it at the schema level instead:

-- COMMAND ----------

GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA tpcdi_dbsql_10 TO `account users`

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC Once again, let's review the permissions on the `dimcustomer` table:

-- COMMAND ----------

SHOW GRANTS ON tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimcustomer

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC Note that all users still have `SELECT` directly on the table itself as a left-over from the previous section. But note also that it is now also granted via the containing schema. Let's check one of the other dimension tables:

-- COMMAND ----------

SHOW GRANTS ON tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimaccount

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC Notice how all users also have `SELECT`, inherited from the containing schema. Not only does `SELECT` apply to all tables in the schema now, but it will also to any *future* tables that are created in this schema.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Privilege inheritance makes it easy to perform bulk grants within Unity Catalog to make permission administration simpler; but you must always be mindful that you do not over-grant permissions using this approach. Following the fundamental rules of least privilege will help here.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Revoking permissions
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC No security model would be complete without the ability to revoke permissions. In SQL, this is done through the `REVOKE` statement.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC As an example, let's reverse the `SELECT` grant on the `tpcdi_dbsql_10` schema.

-- COMMAND ----------

REVOKE SELECT ON SCHEMA tpcdi_dbsql_10 FROM `account users`

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC Now let's recheck permissions on the *dimaccount* table:

-- COMMAND ----------

SHOW GRANTS ON tpcdi_dbsql_10.dimaccount

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC No one else could potentially read from this table now, because the inherited `SELECT` grant has been revoked. We have effectively reverted now to the explicit privilege model.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Catalog Explorer
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Catalog Explorer is a powerful component of the Databricks Data Intelligence Platform, allowing you to interactively browse and manage your data assets from one place.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Open the [Catalog Explorer](/explore/data) and select your personal catalog (as output in the setup cell).
-- MAGIC 1. Select the *tpcdi_dbsql_10* schema.
-- MAGIC 1. Select a table. For this example, we use the *dimcustomer* table.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Examining table metadata and information
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The **Overview** tab shows information about the table:
-- MAGIC * Summary of the table's schema
-- MAGIC * Administrative information like size and number of files
-- MAGIC * Time of last update, giving an indicator of how fresh the data is
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Table metadata, like tags and comments, and greatly enhance your data catalog and make it more manageable. To assist in this regard, Databricks provides AI-suggested comments to help users understand the data.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Overview tab](Includes/images/exploringdata/overview.png)

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Sampling table data
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The **Sample Data** tab provides a peek at the table data. This view can be filtered and sorted if needed.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Sample Data tab](Includes/images/exploringdata/sampledata.png)

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Data governance
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The **Permissions** tab provides an easy way to manage access control of tables and all the data assets in your metastore, equivalent to using `GRANT` and `REVOKE` statements in SQL.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Permissions tab](Includes/images/exploringdata/permissions.png)

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Table history
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Understanding how a table has changed over time can be an important aspect of managing a data warehouse. The **History** tab allows you to see what operations have affected a table over time.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![History tab](Includes/images/exploringdata/history.png)

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC #### Lineage
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The **Lineage** tab provides a view into the table's lineage. Data lineage captures and visualizes in near real-time the lineage of your data as it moves and transforms across different objects such as tables, notebooks, dashboards and more. Lineage is available down to the column level. This allows you to gain insights into your data's origins, as well as the potential downstream impacts of changes to your pipelines.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Lineage tab](Includes/images/exploringdata/lineage.png)

